# Code quality

## Scope

- Change only what the task requires. No unrelated refactors, renames, or “while I’m here” cleanups.
- Prefer editing existing files over adding new ones when either works.
- Do not add docs/markdown/README unless asked.

## Style

- Match surrounding code: naming, imports, formatting, abstraction level.
- Prefer clear names over clever ones. Avoid one-letter names except short loops/callbacks.
- Keep functions small; extract only when it improves clarity or reuse.
- No unnecessary comments. Comment *why*, not *what*. Delete dead code instead of commenting it out.

## Correctness

- Handle errors and empty/loading states where users or callers will notice.
- Don’t swallow errors silently.
- Avoid speculative features (“just in case”) and unused parameters/exports.

## Verification

- After substantive edits, check for obvious type/lint issues in touched files.
- Prefer running the project’s existing checks over inventing new ones.
